SMALLWARE:

PLEASE READ CAREFULLY:
Smallware is a C++ program that showcases how a very small amount of code can recursively delete the contents of a directory, 
illustrating how very little it takes to produce catastrophic effects when executed with sufficient permissions.

The goal of this demonstration is to raise awareness about filesystem safety, 
least‑privilege design, and the importance of secure coding practices. 
The program is intentionally destructive and should only be reviewed and used for educational or research purposes.

Do not run this code on any system containing real, valuable, or personal data.  
Do not distribute or share this code to anyone in favor of carrying out an attack.
Execute only in a controlled, disposable environment such as a virtual machine.
It is YOUR responsibility to ensure that this code is used safely, professionally, and educationally.

Download all of the files located in the Smallware directory, then put them inside a dedicated folder on your device.
Once you have created a Makefile or ran g++, execute it. It should not take very long for the program to finish.
Upon rebooting your VM or test environment, there is a possibility that the system may not complete its boot cycle
provided a working directory fed to the program is sufficient enough.
